{
  "dom_id": "step-q35663",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)\\in S,$ where $f(x)=2x.$\n- For every $p(x),q(x)\\in S,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in S.$\n- The set is closed under addition.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$ However, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(2x)=1\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2x\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $S$ such that their sum is *not* in $S.$ For example, let $p(x)=x+{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=x-{x}^{2}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $S$ since $\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(q(x))=2\\ge 2.$ However,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(p(x)+q(x))=\\operatorname{deg}\u2061((x+{x}^{2})+(x-{x}^{2}))=\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(2x)=1\u22712.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"",
